plementation before tests—format should come first since it's a foundational package. I need to check what requirements are actually specified in the PRDs to anchor what tests should cover, but I'm working only with the context provided here. I'm narrowing down which package to test first. pkg/format/ is the best choice since it's self-contained with no external dependencies, and the functions are deterministic. Looking at the PRD requirements for format, I need to cover table alignment, column padding, display width calculations, and ANSI color handling.\n\nI'll organize the test requirements into three groups: table formatting functions (Columns, PadRight, PadLeft, displayWidth), ANSI color functions (FileTypeColor, Reset, ColorEnabled, SetColorEnabled, ResetColorEnabled), and human-readable size formatting (HumanSize).\n\nI'm planning three test files—one for table formatting, one for color functions, and one for size formatting—which should total around 240 lines, fitting within the target range. Since I'm limited to exactly one task, I need to write a clear task description that includes the requirement IDs in the title.\n\nFor the required reading, the stitch agent will need to reference the PRD document at docs/specs/product-requirements/prd003-format to understand the requirements, along with the existing source code to know what to test.\n\nLooking at the project's test conventions, I see the pattern: test files mirror their source counterparts, tests use table-driven patterns following Go standards, and color tests manage TTY detection through helper functions. I should focus on what's actually derived from the codebase rather than inventing patterns.\n\nThe key design decision I can extract is using SetColorEnabled and ResetColorEnabled to control color output during testing—this comes directly from the PRD requirements for TTY override behavior. The file mirroring convention is established in the architecture, so that's worth noting. Now I'm looking at what specific test cases need to be written for the format functions.\n\nI'm estimating the test coverage: table tests handling various column scenarios and padding functions around 80-100 lines, color tests covering file type colors and the enable/reset behavior around 80-100 lines, and size tests for human-readable byte formatting around 50-70 lines. That puts us at roughly 210-270 lines total, which is slightly ambitious but reasonable. I should also verify the acceptance criteria align with the project standards—3-5 criteria per the guidelines, and I have 3 requirements which fits the constraint. Let me draft the acceptance criteria now, starting with the test execution requirement and then covering the specific test coverage areas.\n\nNow I'm refining the acceptance criteria to ensure they're concrete and measurable. I need to map this task back to the specific use case reference and check if there's an existing test suite specification document I should include in the required reading section. I'm laying out the test file structure with specific test cases for each formatting function—table operations, color handling, and size conversion—organized by their corresponding source files and linked to the product requirements. I need to verify the size formatting handles decimal places correctly across both binary and SI unit systems, then confirm the test suite runs successfully. Verifying the test specification meets all requirements: single task with three acceptance criteria covering table layout, color handling, and size formatting across different modes, totaling around 200-250 lines of test code anchored to the format requirements, properly scoped to pkg/format/, with all required fields and mappings in place.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":46346,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":10,"servic